1 Getting started

1.1 What is coole?

   coole is a console text editor, a continuation of cooledit by Paul
   Sheer.  It has syntax highlighting, code
   folding, an undo history browser, macros and a macro explorer,
   ctags and etags navigation, spell checking, a user menu, multiple
   windows, editor plugins and Lua scripting.

   The program and the package are called coole.  Run it as

     coole [+lineno] file1[:lineno] [file2[:lineno]...]

1.2 Where does it come from?

   coole continues cooledit, the editor written by Paul Sheer.  The
   terminal version of cooledit was developed for many years as the
   internal editor of GNU Midnight Commander; coole takes it with the
   fixes and features it gained there and develops it as a program of
   its own.  It is not an official GNU package.

   It keeps the keys and the file formats of that editor.

   Report problems with coole here, not to the upstream project, and do
   not report problems of the original program to us.

1.3 Does it run on my machine?

   coole runs on POSIX (Unix-like) operating systems, such as
   GNU/Linux, FreeBSD, macOS, Solaris and others.

   It uses GNU Autoconf, which should configure it for every Unix
   clone.

1.4 Does it work with my terminal?

   Yes, it does.

   Because this is a full screen program it doesn't run on dummy
   terminals, but anything more advanced will do (like vt100).  If your
   terminal works with vi, emacs, elm or pine it will work here.

1.5 What else do I need to run it?

   You need a POSIX (Unix compatible) operating system and glib >= 2.58,
   available at https://download.gnome.org/sources/glib/ .

   If you want to use the mouse on the Linux console you need the gpm
   daemon from https://www.nico.schottelius.org/software/gpm/ .  You
   need nothing extra to use the mouse on xterm.

   The screen is drawn through the S-Lang library or through ncurses;
   "coole -V" says which one this build uses.  Editor plugins and the
   Lua runtime need GModule, which comes with glib.  Everything else is
   optional and is listed in the INSTALL file.

1.6 Is coole Public Domain? Copyrighted?

   It is under the GNU General Public License, which basically means
   that you may freely copy, change and distribute it, but that you may
   not impose any restrictions on further distribution, and that you
   must make the source code available.  This is not the same as Public
   Domain.  The license is included in the source distribution (the
   COPYING file).

   Most of the code belongs to the Free Software Foundation, to which
   its authors gave their rights.  The
   code written for coole belongs to its own authors, listed in the
   AUTHORS file.

1.7 Where can I get coole?

   The development version is in the master branch of

     https://github.com/blue-panels/coole

1.8 I cannot compile it.  What should I do?

   Make sure you have read the INSTALL file in the sources.  Report the
   error messages exactly as they appear, mention the versions of your
   OS, your compiler and whatever else software you think is relevant.


2 Keyboard

2.1 What does documentation mean with the C-?, M-? and F? keys?

   The documentation uses emacs style names for keyboard keys.

   C stands for the Ctrl key.  For example, C-f means that you should
   hold down the Ctrl key and press the f key.

   M stands for the Meta key.  Your terminal might call it Alt or
   Compose instead of Meta.  For example, M-f means that you should hold
   down the Meta/Alt/Compose key and press the f key.  If your terminal
   doesn't have Meta, Alt or Compose or they don't work you can use Esc.
   For M-f press the Esc key and then press the f key.

   Sometimes Ctrl and Alt are used instead of C and M for simplicity.
   Keep in mind that Alt can actually be Meta on some keyboards.

   F? stands for a function key.  If your terminal doesn't have function
   keys or they don't work you can use Esc.  For example, for F3 press
   the Esc key and then press the 3 key.

2.2 Why don't function keys (or some other key) work?

   Your terminfo or termcap database has missing or incorrect
   definitions for function keys.  Type "coole -V" to see what terminal
   database is being used.

   Up-to-date termcap and terminfo databases are available here:

     http://www.catb.org/~esr/terminfo/
     https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/ncurses/

   If you don't have permission to edit terminal databases, teach the
   program the key instead: Options -> Learn keys, press the key and
   save.  What it learns is kept per terminal type in
   ~/.config/coole/term/$TERM, so a key learned in one terminal does not
   disturb another.

   Options -> Key sniffer shows the bytes your terminal really sends,
   which is the fastest way to find out what is going on.

   If all else fails you can emulate function keys by first pressing the
   ESC key and then one of the number keys.  For example, if you want to
   produce F9, press ESC, then 9.  If you don't have an ESC key on your
   keyboard you can try alt-9 or meta-9.

2.3 How do I use function keys F11 to F20?

   These are normally mapped to function keys F1 to F10 with Shift held,
   e.g. function key F13 can be activated by pressing Shift-F3.  You can
   define the keys this way in the Options -> Learn keys dialog.  The
   convention for PC keyboards is that F11-20 always means Shift with
   F1-10.

   You may find that on the Linux console with some keyboard layouts
   Shift-Fn is interpreted as F(n+12), not as F(n+10).  This is a result
   of non-uniformity of keyboard layouts.  Use "Learn keys" to remedy
   this inconvenience.

2.4 Why does the ESC key behave funny?

   The ESC key is a prefix for simulating the Meta and Alt keys (for
   terminals which don't have Meta or Alt, see the three previous
   questions).  For example, pressing ESC-a is the same as pressing
   Meta-a.  In addition most terminals use ESC for internal
   representation of arrow keys, function keys and other enhanced keys.
   If you want to use ESC to cancel things you have to press it twice,
   i.e. ESC-ESC.  If you find this cumbersome you can generally use F10
   to cancel.  Alternatively turn on the old_esc_mode setting in the
   ~/.config/coole/ini file.  The old_esc_mode setting makes ESC work as a
   prefix only if another key is pressed within 0.5 seconds.  After 0.5
   seconds the ESC key cancels.  There is no way to make ESC cancel
   immediately (if we want to be able to use arrow keys and function
   keys).

2.5 Some Ctrl key doesn't work!

   Maybe the key is a stty control character on your terminal (C-s,
   C-q and C-z often are).  See man stty for details on how to list and
   change stty control characters.

2.6 What 'keys' are the "a1" and "c1" keys mentioned in the manual?

   A1 is the "Home" key on the keypad (numpad 7 with Num Lock turned
   off), which is decoded as a different key from the regular "Home".
   Likewise C1 is keypad "End", A3 is keypad "Page Up", C3 is keypad
   "Page Down", and B2 is documented to "center of keypad", i.e. the 5
   key, though I don't know whether any keyboards actually use that.
   They are documented for the curses library (search for "keypad"):

   https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/007908799/xcurses/curses.h.html

2.7 How do I change the key bindings?

   Options -> Key bindings lists every command with the keys it answers
   to, and rebinds them on the spot.  What you change there is written
   to ~/.config/coole/keymap.ini.

   The same file can be written by hand, and so can the system-wide
   /etc/coole/keymap.ini; the coole(1) man page describes the format.

2.8 What does my terminal really send for this key?

   Options -> Key sniffer shows the raw bytes of every key you press,
   together with the name the program gives it.  Use it when a key does
   nothing, or when two keys look the same to the program.


3 Mouse

3.1 How do I enable mouse support?

   Invoke coole like this (without quotes): "coole -x".  If this doesn't
   work upgrade to a terminal which is compatible with the Xterm mouse
   sequences.

   Alternatively, on the Linux console you can use gpm.

3.2 How do I cut and paste text with mouse?

   Hold down the shift key while using the mouse to cut and paste.


4 Display

4.1 Why do I keep getting "Terminal not powerful enough for SLang" or
    "Terminal not powerful enough for SLsmg"?

   This means that your terminfo databases do not contain the correct
   definitions for your terminal.

   You could try using a different terminal setting.  If you use csh or
   tcsh:

     setenv TERM vt100

   or if you use sh, bash, ksh or zsh:

     export TERM=vt100

4.2 Why don't line drawing characters work?

   The command line option -a forces the use of the characters +, |, -
   for line drawing.  Use it if none of the suggestions below helps.

   In general, there are three cases:
     * Lines are shown as ASCII characters like this

      +---------+
      |         |
      +---------+

       This also happens when you use the -a option.  Other than that
       possible reason is 1 or 2 (see below).

     * Lines are shown as lower case characters like this

      lqqqqqqqqqk
      x         x
      mqqqqqqqqqj

       Possible reason is 1 or 2 (see below).

     * Lines are shown as blanks or missing characters.  Possible reason
       is 2 or 3 (see below).

   The reason for the problem is one of following:

    1. Your terminal might not support line drawing characters.  VT100
       compatible terminals, rxvt and xterm and color_xterm do support
       them.
    2. Your terminfo or termcap database might have missing or incorrect
       definitions for line drawing characters.  Set the acsc variable
       in the terminfo database like this:
       acsc=a\376k\277l\332m\300j\331n\305w\302v\301u\264t\303q\304x
       \263h\2600\333
       Don't forget to issue the 'tic' command.  This supposes you are
       using the PC character set.  The octal values might be different
       for other character sets.  If you are using termcap instead of
       terminfo, you should modify the above solution appropriately.
    3. Your terminal font might not support line drawing characters.
       Try changing the font.

4.3 Can one use latin-1 characters without losing the lines?

   Yes, you need a correct font and a correct termcap/terminfo database.

   For font, if you use xterm try "xterm -fn fixed".

   For termcap/terminfo database, change the acsc capability in the
   database.

4.4 How can I get colors?

   Invoke coole like this (without quotes): "coole -c".

   If you get colors, be happy.

   If your terminal stays black and white, your terminal doesn't support
   color.  You might want to upgrade to a terminal which is compatible
   with the ANSI color sequences.

   If your terminal goes completely black, see the next question.

   More detailed answer:

   Check that your terminal supports color.  Most terminals do. You can
   test color support with the following command:

        printf "\033[32m Hello color world! \033[m\n"

   You can test 256 color support with the following command:

        printf "\033[38;5;120m Hello 256-color world! \033[m\n"

   You can test true color support with the following command:

        printf "\033[38;2;0;200;0m Hello true color world! \033[m\n"

   If you see the text in green, your terminal supports the respective
   color mode.

   Check that you are using the proper TERM variable for your terminal.
   If not, set it accordingly. You can use the 'toe -a' command to list
   all available terminfo entries.

   If your terminal supports 256 colors, the correct entry name may be
   appended with -256color. If your terminal supports true color, it
   should be appended with -direct, -direct16, or -direct256. All three
   variants provide both 256-color and true color support simultaneously.
   The latter two variants make no difference here, and they support
   basic colors including their bright versions along with true color
   support, in contrast to the -direct variant, which supports only basic
   8 colors together with true color when used in a skin.

   If there is no 256-color or true-color terminfo variant for your
   terminal, even though your terminal supports it, send an e-mail to
   ncurses/terminfo maintainers (bug-ncurses@gnu.org) and ask them to
   add one.

   With the S-Lang library (you can check by 'coole -V'), you can force
   color support by setting the environment variable COLORTERM to any
   value, and specifically force true color support by setting it to
   'truecolor'.

4.5 My color_xterm goes completely (or partially) black!

   Some color_xterm terminals define all colors as black instead of the
   standard ANSI colors.  This makes them go completely black when you
   try to use colors.

   You will have to override the defaults.  Create a file
   "color.defaults" which has the following contents:

    color_xterm*color0:           Black
    color_xterm*color1:           Red
    color_xterm*color2:           Green
    color_xterm*color3:           Yellow
    color_xterm*color4:           Blue
    color_xterm*color5:           Magenta
    color_xterm*color6:           Cyan
    color_xterm*color7:           White
    color_xterm*background:       White
    color_xterm*foreground:       Black

   (replace color_xterm with the name of your color_xterm, color_xterm
   mentions its name in its title bar)

   Now type:

    xrdb -merge color.defaults

   Alternatively you can add the suggested contents of the
   color.defaults file to your .Xdefaults or .Xresources file (or what
   ever the name of your X configuration file is).  Or you can replace
   your non-ANSI color_xterm with an ANSI color_xterm.

4.6 I got colors working here but the other programs don't work at all
anymore!

   This program uses the terminfo database (if available) but many other
   programs use the termcap database.  If you set the TERM environment
   variable to a value which has no corresponding entry in the termcap
   database those programs stop working.  You should add the new value
   of TERM to the termcap database.

   Example: If you have set TERM to xterm-color locate from /etc/termcap
   the line which starts:

    xterm|vs100|xterm terminal emulator

   Change it to start:

    xterm|xterm-color|vs100|xterm terminal emulator

4.7 How do I change the colors of the program itself?

   Colors come from a skin file.  Options -> Appearance lists the skins
   that are installed and applies one at once; the choice is kept in
   ~/.config/coole/ini.  "coole -S name" picks a skin for one run.

   System skins live in /usr/share/coole/skins, your own in
   ~/.local/share/coole/skins.  A skin is a plain ini file, so copying
   one of the system skins into your own directory and editing it is the
   usual way to make your own.

4.8 Why is the program linked with X libraries?

   It reads key modifiers from the X server, which helps to tell apart
   keys that the terminal emulator reports in the same way, e.g. PgUp
   and Ctrl-PgUp.  The libraries are loaded dynamically on the systems
   that support it, and "coole -V" says whether this build has the
   support at all.  "coole -X" turns it off for one run.


5 Editing

5.1 When I try pasting, the amount of indenting increases with each
line!

   Turn off 'Return does autoindent' in the editor options, or hold
   Shift while you paste, which suspends the autoindent.

5.2 How do I add syntax highlighting for my language?

   The rules are in /usr/share/coole/syntax, one file per language, and
   the file Syntax there says which of them a file name gets.  To add or
   change a rule, copy the syntax directory to
   ~/.local/share/coole/syntax, edit your copy, and the editor takes it
   instead of the system one.

   The editor also has code folding (Command -> Fold / Unfold block),
   an undo history on Alt-Shift-u and a macro explorer on Alt-Shift-m;
   a filter on Alt-s hides every line that does not match, which is a
   quick way through a log.

5.3 Is there any way to include additional commands?

   Yes, F11 invokes a user menu, which is fully configurable.  You can
   add any shell commands to it.  The editor reads .coole.menu in the
   current directory, then ~/.config/coole/menu, then the system-wide
   /etc/coole/coole.menu; Options -> Menu file edits it.  The coole(1)
   man page describes the format.

   Editor plugins and Lua scripts add commands too, see 5.4.

   And finally, you can code any feature you want yourself.  The source
   code is free, which means you can change it any way you want.  There
   are some limitations to make sure it stays free.  See the GNU General
   Public License for details.

5.4 What are editor plugins and Lua scripts?

   An editor plugin is a shared library that adds commands to the
   editor menus, for example the ctags source navigator.  The ctags,
   etags and spell plugins are built into the program; others are
   loaded from $libdir/coole/editor-plugins and from
   ~/.local/lib/coole/editor-plugins.  doc/PLUGINS in the source
   distribution describes how to write one.

   Lua scripts are smaller and need no compiler.  They live in
   /usr/share/coole/lua/scripts and in ~/.local/share/coole/lua/scripts,
   and a script with the same name in your own directory shadows the
   system one.  doc/LUA_PLUGINS.md and doc/LUA_API_REFERENCE.md describe
   what they can do.  "coole --no-lua" starts without them.

5.5 How do I turn a plugin off, or configure it?

   Options -> Manage plugins lists the plugins, with a switch for each.
   What you turn off there is not loaded at the next start; the state
   is kept in ~/.config/coole/plugins.ini.

   A plugin that has settings of its own opens them from that same
   dialog with Enter or F4.


6 Configuration files

6.1 Where does the program keep my settings?

   In three directories, following the XDG layout:

     ~/.config/coole        ini, keymap.ini, menu, learned keys,
                            plugin settings
     ~/.local/share/coole   skins, syntax, Lua scripts, editor files
     ~/.cache/coole         data that can be thrown away

   Editor plugins of your own live in ~/.local/lib/coole.  "coole -F"
   prints every one of these paths, which is the quickest way to see
   where a particular file went.


7 Other common problems

7.1 When I start the program, nothing happens!

   One reason is problems with gpm.  Try the "--nomouse" option to
   see if it makes any difference.  Restarting gpm can help.  Sometimes
   it's enough to move the mouse.

   Also, if you have the DISPLAY environment variable set, but the X
   server is unreachable (e.g. it's firewalled), this can also cause a
   delay at startup.  Unset DISPLAY, or run "coole -X", to see if that's
   the case.


8 Development

8.1 Who has written this?

   coole is maintained by Ilia Maslakov.  It continues cooledit, written
   by Paul Sheer, and the work of the many people who developed that
   editor since.  The AUTHORS file lists them all.

8.2 Do I dare to use a development version?

   Yes, of course.  The master branch of

     https://github.com/blue-panels/coole

   is where the work happens; check it out and build it as the INSTALL
   file describes.

   But always remember: development versions may have nasty bugs at some
   points of time.  It's up to you to judge whether the new features and
   fixes for the bugs you know outweigh the risk of unknown bugs.

8.3 How can I report a bug/request for a feature?

   You might first want to get the newest development version to see if
   the bug is fixed or the feature is added already.

   If this is not the case, open a ticket at

     https://github.com/blue-panels/coole/issues

   Bugs of coole do not belong to the upstream project, and bugs of
   the upstream program do not belong here.

   Give as many details as possible.  A too long message is a lot better
   than a too short message.  Say which version you run ("coole -V"),
   which terminal and which operating system.

   For segmentation faults a stack backtrace is appreciated.  You can
   produce a stack backtrace as follows:
     * If the segmentation fault produced a core file:
         1. Load the core file by typing "gdb coole core".
         2. Type "where".
         3. Cut and paste the results into your report.
     * If the segmentation fault didn't produce a core file:
         1. Load coole by typing "gdb coole".
         2. Start coole by typing "run".
         3. Try to reproduce the segmentation fault by doing whatever
            you did last time when the segmentation fault occurred.
         4. Type "where".
         5. Cut and paste the results into your report.
         6. For the future you might want to check out what the command
            in your shell is to allow producing of core files.  Usually
            it is "ulimit -c unlimited".

8.4 How can I join the development?

   Send a pull request on GitHub, or open a ticket with the patch.
   Before you start coding, check the latest development version; it
   might be that your feature has already been implemented.  The HACKING
   file describes how the source is laid out and how it is formatted.

   The code you write stays yours.  coole asks for no copyright
   assignment.

8.5 How can I help with translations?

   The program strings are in the po files under po/, and the manual
   pages are in doc/man; send changes to them as a pull request.


9 More information

9.1 This document didn't answer my question.  Where else can I look
for an answer?

   Press F1: the built-in help is the manual page, and it covers nearly
   everything.  The wiki has the longer guides:

     https://github.com/blue-panels/coole/wiki

   Upgrade to a newer version.  Many problems are fixed in the new
   versions.

   If you still can't find an answer, ask in a ticket at
   https://github.com/blue-panels/coole/issues .

9.2 Where should I look on the World Wide Web?

   The project lives at

     https://github.com/blue-panels/coole


10 Legal issues

